#set page(width: 220pt, height: 108pt, margin: 16pt, numbering: "1")
#set text(size: 9pt)

The survey began at the lighthouse.#footnote[Notes were taken at dawn on the first of the month, from the gallery.] The keeper had left a log on the table and a kettle on the stove, and both were still warm when the first of the party arrived at the door.

Heavy cloud sat low over the water all morning.#footnote[A second note, long enough to take several lines of its own at the foot of the page, so that it competes with the text above for the room that is left, and a good deal of it carries over onto the following page when the paragraph that cites it falls near the bottom.] Nothing moved on the sea except a single trawler working north.

By midday the wind had dropped, and the party walked the length of the breakwater.#footnote[The breakwater is about four hundred metres long.] They counted the stones on the seaward side, the cracked ones and the sound ones, and wrote the figures in the margin of the log.

The afternoon went to the old signal station.#footnote[Built in the same decade as the lighthouse, it was never fitted with a lamp.] Its door had been painted shut, and the shutters were rotten through, so the party looked in from outside and moved on without comment.

Evening brought a change in the weather.#footnote[A third long note, which gives the reading of the barometer hour by hour through the evening and the night, and which therefore runs to several lines and is expected to spill over the foot of its page into the page that follows it.] Rain came in from the west and the light on the water went out.

The last paragraph closes the account of the first day.#footnote[Short.] Tomorrow the party will go inland, to the village and the church.
